# Configuration example for Bash_WP-CLI_Update.sh 6.2.0
#
# Copy it to one of these places and edit:
#
#   /etc/wp-cli-update.conf            global, read by every invocation
#   <script dir>/wp-cli-update.conf    per installation, wins over the global one
#
# ...or point at any file with --config FILE.
#
# The file is DATA, not shell. Only plain KEY=VALUE lines are read; the file is
# never sourced. A line containing a backtick, $( , a pipe, a semicolon, an
# ampersand or a redirection makes the whole file be rejected with exit code 4,
# because a configuration file that can execute code is a privilege escalation
# waiting for a loose permission bit.
#
# Precedence, lowest to highest:
#   built-in defaults < /etc/wp-cli-update.conf < ./wp-cli-update.conf
#                     < WP_CLI_UPDATE_<KEY> environment < command line
#
# Run `Bash_WP-CLI_Update.sh --print-config` to see the effective value of every
# setting together with the layer that produced it.
#
# Permissions: keep this file readable by root only. It can hold a licence.
#   chmod 600 wp-cli-update.conf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- executables
# Where the wp binary lives. Do not rely on PATH: the manager switches users,
# and the login shell of a site owner usually has a different PATH than yours.
# If you set this, it must be an executable file -- there is no silent fallback.
WP_CLI_PATH=/usr/local/bin/wp

# The discovery script, run automatically when the site list is missing.
#DISCOVER_SCRIPT=/opt/wp-cli-update/Find_WP_Senior.sh

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ site list
# One absolute path per line. Blank lines and lines starting with '#' are
# ignored, a trailing CR is tolerated, and leading/trailing whitespace is
# trimmed. Produced by Find_WP_Senior.sh.
SITES_FILE=/var/lib/wp-cli-update/wp-found.txt

# Run the discovery script when the list is missing or empty. Set to 0 on
# production hosts: an automatic scan of the default web roots costs I/O and can
# pick up a site you did not mean to touch.
AUTO_DISCOVER=1

# Process at most this many sites per run, 0 for all. Useful when you are
# rolling a change out in batches.
MAX_SITES=0

# Skip these plugins on plugin/theme operations (comma separated, the WP-CLI
# syntax). The default is EMPTY on purpose: a list that somebody's site needed
# must not silently ship inside a general tool. Put your own here.
#SKIP_PLUGINS=

# Also pass --skip-plugins to listing commands. Off by default, because it hides
# exactly the plugins you are listing.
SKIP_PLUGINS_FOR_LISTING=0

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------- logs
LOG_FILE=/var/log/wp-cli-update/manager.log
ERROR_LOG_FILE=/var/log/wp-cli-update/errors.log

# Rotate when a log grows past LOG_MAX_BYTES, keeping LOG_KEEP generations
# (manager.log.1 ... manager.log.N). 0 disables rotation.
LOG_MAX_BYTES=5242880
LOG_KEEP=3

# debug | info | warn | error
LOG_LEVEL=info

# How many lines of a failing wp command are printed to the console and written
# to the error log.
ERROR_OUTPUT_LINES=20

# auto | always | never. `auto` emits colour only when stdout and stderr are both
# terminals and NO_COLOR is unset, so logs and CI output stay clean.
COLOR=auto

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- locks
# Prevents two runs from updating the same sites at the same time. flock(1) is
# used when present, a pid file otherwise.
LOCK_FILE=/var/run/wp-cli-update.lock

# --------------------------------------------------------------------- safety
# Per-command timeout in seconds, 0 to disable. A hung wp process otherwise
# blocks the whole fleet run until someone notices.
TIMEOUT=600

# Signal sent when the timeout expires, and seconds to wait before escalating to
# SIGKILL. WP-CLI ignores TERM in some database operations, hence the escalation.
TIMEOUT_SIGNAL=TERM
KILL_AFTER=30

# When to pass --allow-root to WP-CLI:
#   auto    only when the manager itself runs as root (the normal case)
#   always  always
#   never   never
ALLOW_ROOT=auto

# When the run exits non-zero:
#   any     at least one site failed            (default; right for cron alerts)
#   all     every site failed                   (right for "best effort" jobs)
#   never   always 0                            (right when you parse the log)
FAIL_ON=any

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- Astra
# The add-on slug.
ASTRA_SLUG=astra-addon

# The licence value. Prefer the environment (WP_CLI_UPDATE_LICENCE) or a key
# file over this one, so the value does not sit in a configuration file:
#
#   export WP_CLI_UPDATE_LICENCE=...            # per invocation
#   ./astra.key                                 # next to the script, chmod 600
#   /etc/wp-cli-update/astra.key                # host-wide, chmod 600
#   ~/.astra.key
#
# The value is never passed as an argument. It is written to a temporary file
# that the child shell reads at run time, so it appears neither in `ps` output
# nor in any log line. Legacy names ASTRA_KEY and ASTRA_LICENSE_KEY are also
# read from the environment.
#LICENCE=

# ------------------------------------------------------------- user switching
# Space separated list of variable NAMES to pass through into the site owner's
# environment (for example a proxy or an API endpoint). Only the names are
# listed here; the values are taken from the manager's own environment.
#USER_ENV=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- parallelism
# Process N sites at a time. 1 (the default) is sequential.
#
# This is a batch barrier, not a continuous pool: bash 4.2 has no `wait -n`, so
# the next batch waits for the slowest site of the current one. Pick N from the
# slowest shared resource, which is normally the database server and not the CPU
# -- 4 to 8 is a sane range for one MySQL instance on one host.
#
# While a batch runs, per-site output and log lines are buffered and replayed in
# site order at the barrier. `tail -f` on the log therefore looks stalled until
# the batch lands; this is deliberate, interleaved lines from concurrent sites
# are unreadable.
JOBS=1

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- backups
# off | db | full
#   db    `wp db export` before the site is touched. Seconds, and it covers
#         everything these modes change.
#   full  tar.gz of the whole installation. Complete, and on a site with a large
#         wp-content/uploads it is tens of gigabytes and many minutes; the script
#         warns with the tree size before archiving.
# Off by default on purpose: a dump per site per run fills disks nobody monitors.
BACKUP=off
BACKUP_DIR=/var/backups/wp-cli-update
# Backups kept per site, 0 = keep everything. Pruned after each new backup.
KEEP_BACKUPS=3

# ------------------------------------------------------- plugin selection
# Update only active plugins that actually have an update available. The set is
# enumerated from `plugin list --format=json` and passed by slug, so one broken
# plugin cannot hide behind `--all`.
ONLY_ACTIVE=0

# Comma separated slugs or names to leave out of --plugins/--full. Matching is
# case-insensitive against both the slug and the display name, and it is a whole
# token comparison -- never a pattern, so nothing here needs escaping.
#EXCLUDE_PLUGINS=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- multisite
# Passed to WP-CLI as --url on every call. Needed when one directory serves
# several sites and the operation must target one of them.
#URL=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- reporting
# Exit non-zero when anything was warned about, not only when something failed.
# This is what makes a warning visible to cron and to CI.
STRICT=0

# Run WP-CLI as the invoking user instead of switching into the site owner.
# For single-site hosts where the operator already is the site user, and for
# test suites. Leave it off on a multi-tenant host: the switch is the point.
NO_USER_SWITCH=0
